the cuban authorities have prevented the  country's most prominent opposition  leader junior garcia from leaving his  home in havana for a solo protest  the operation came the day before  marches planned across the country to  demand greater freedoms and the release  of political prisoners a protest the  government has declared illegal  garcia said that every cuban should have  the right to demonstrate peacefully  i think that is my greatest aspiration  that every cuban knows they have power a  lot of power that a single citizen can  make a system tremble  the cuban government has organised a  number of counter demonstrations and  last week called hundreds of foreign  diplomats to a meeting to accuse the  united states of being behind the  protest  washington's representatives in havana  were not invited  and this video aired on state television  accuses yunya garcia  of working with the u.s authorities and  anti-cuban organizations  claims he strongly denies  the planned marches on monday coincide  with cuba reopening its doors to foreign  tourism  closed for 20 months because of the  covet 19 pandemic  restaurant owner carlos marquez says the  return of tourism will help the  country's battered economy he called the  opposition demonstration a distraction  i'm sure it won't happen sure  sure from the moment they started  planning it it was a crazy idea  tens of thousands of cubans think  otherwise they belong to archipelago a  movement organized on social media it  grew out of protest in july which took  the authorities by surprise due to their  size and spontaneity hundreds were  arrested  [Music]  growing dissent is being expressed in  other ways too this video by musician  roberto carcasses is called no one can  stop me  authorities will be better prepared to  repel monday's planned marches and the  threat of violence will keep many away  although he said even if the protest is  suppressed the opposition movement will  keep growing  for the first time in nearly all the  provinces in the country the people have  come together to cry for freedom that's  historic symbolically it marks the  beginning of the end  it's a prediction made prematurely many  times since fidel castro came to power  in 1959.  the difference this time is the  protesters are staying in cuba to fight  for change rather than leaving and  that's a challenge the cuban authorities  are struggling to deal with  daniel schweinler al jazeera
 

